test_elf — ELF64 parser selftest
=================================

Phase 1 chunk 7 stand-in for "load a real ELF and run it." The
kernel ships an `oskit.lib.elf` parser this chunk; the actual
"build a sysl program as ELF, ship it in the ramdisk, exec() it"
path arrives with chunk 8 (`exec`). Until then this test drives
`SYS_ELF_SELFTEST` (92), which builds a synthetic two-LOAD-segment
ELF in kernel memory, runs every `oskit.lib.elf` accessor against
it, and returns 0 on full pass or 1..N for the failing step.

What this test would fail-on if regressed:

  * `elf64_validate` accepting a bad magic or wrong class — caught
    by step 1 (positive validate) or step 14 (negative magic).
  * `elf64_pf_to_vma_prot` aliasing PF_X (1) onto PROT_READ (1) —
    caught by step 8 (R+X expected to map to PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC=5).
  * Off-by-one in the per-PHT-entry stride (`phoff + i*phentsize`)
    — caught by steps 9-13 (second PHT entry's fields).
  * Mis-sized truncation check — caught by step 15.

Pass criterion: prints `elf: ok`. The fail() helper exits with the
step number so a regression is greppable from the test output.

    module oskit.bin.test_elf

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall6}


    val SYS_ELF_SELFTEST = 92


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("elf: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    main(argv: []string)
        val rc = syscall6(SYS_ELF_SELFTEST, i64(0), i64(0),
                          i64(0), i64(0), i64(0), i64(0))
        if rc != i64(0)
            fail(int(rc))
            return

        puts("elf: ok\n")
        exit()
